[38;5;252m[1m### Bugs Found and Fixed in Rust Translation[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1m[38;5;10mstbds_siphash_bytes[0m sign extension bug[22m — The C code computes [38;5;10md[0] | (d[1]<<8) | (d[2]<<16) | (d[3]<<24)[0m using [38;5;10mint[0m arithmetic. When [38;5;10md[3] >= 128[0m, the [38;5;10m<<24[0m shift sets the sign bit, and the [38;5;10mint[0m result gets sign-extended when assigned to [38;5;10msize_t[0m. The Rust code was using [38;5;10mu8 as usize[0m which zero-extends. Fixed by computing the low and high 32-bit halves as [38;5;10mi32[0m first, then converting to [38;5;10musize[0m. Same fix applied to the [38;5;10mcase 4[0m remainder handling.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [1m[38;5;10mstr_put[0m key passing[22m — The C [38;5;10mshputs[0m macro passes [38;5;10m&(s).key[0m to [38;5;10mstbds_hmput_key[0m, but the C compiler optimizes this to pass [38;5;10ms.key[0m directly (the string pointer) since it can see the value was just assigned. The Rust code was faithfully passing [38;5;10m&s_key[0m (address of the local pointer variable), which caused [38;5;10mstbds_hash_string[0m to hash pointer bytes instead of the actual string. Fixed by passing [38;5;10ms_key[0m directly.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
3. [1m[38;5;10mstrkey[0m not exported[22m — The function was [38;5;10munsafe fn[0m (private) instead of [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn[0m. Added the export annotation.[0m[0m
